I'm a reasonable man, get off my case - Radiohead, "Packt Like Sardines In a Crushd Tin Box"

`packt` is a tool for interacting with [blocks] files.

[blocks]: https://github.com/htogta/blocks

```
packt help
packt version
packt new blockfile
packt info [--states] blockfile
packt clean blockfile [--force] [i:[j]]
packt read blockfile [--bytes] [i:[j]] [file]
packt write blockfile [--bytes] [i:[j]] [file]
packt print blockfile [--lines] i[:j]
packt archive blockfile [--bytes] [--dir] [i:[j]] path [path2 ...]
```

Or, for short:

```
packt help
packt version
packt new blockfile
packt info blockfile [-s]
packt clean blockfile [-f] [i:[j]]
packt read blockfile [-b] [i:[j]] [file]
packt write blockfile [-b] [i:[j]] [file]
packt print blockfile [-l] i[:j]
packt archive blockfile [-b] [-d] [i:[j]] path [path2 ...]
```

```
packt blockfile --write [i[:j]] [file]
packt blockfile -w [i[:j]] [file]
```

^ Write bytes from `file` into the blockfile, starting at block `i` and ending after block `j`. If `file` not provided, uses standard input. `i` is 0 by default, and if `j` is not provided it simply writes to the next blocks until the entire file has been written. Add the `--error` flag to halt with an error if any blocks being written to are not already empty (all zeroes) rather than continuing writing- by default, if a block isn't empty, a warning is printed, but this can be disabled with `--quiet`. 

If the number of blocks specified by the range is *not* enough space to write the file's contents, the program will exit with an error. If it's *too* large, subsequent blocks will have zeroes written to them- disable this with `--nozeroes`.

```
packt blockfile --writebytes [i] [file]
packt blockfile -W [i] [file]
```

```
packt blockfile --read i[:j] [file]
packt blockfile -r i[:j] [file]
```

^ Reads data from the blockfile into `file`. If `j` is not provided, only the one block at position `i` is read. If `file` is not provided, data is written to standard output.

packt blockfile --print i[:j]
packt blockfile -p i[:j]

- read file bytes into block, starting at block i and ending at block j

- read block range into a file
- write file as blocks, starting at an index
- write block to stdout (raw)
- read stdin into block starting at index
- pretty-print blocks to stdout
